    {
      "item_id": "36c50723-3b65-49f6-9d9b-70574cc9471c",
      "session_id": "AdditionalQuestions-36c50723-what-happened-to-salander-in-the-abandoned-fa",
      "trace_id": "e514f33b2b8ec8a516ae0bf920c15229",
      "output": {
        "question": "What happened to Salander in the abandoned factory?",
        "answer": "Based on the passages retrieved, Salander was shot by Alexander Zalachenko at the Gosseberga farmhouse (which is distinct from the brickworks where Niedermann later hid). Specifically:\n\n1.  **Location:** The shooting occurred at Zalachenko's farmhouse in Gosseberga, not in an abandoned factory or brickworks. Salander had crawled out of a grave near the farmhouse and entered a woodshed on the property [E1], [E2].\n2.  **The Shooting:** After Salander attacked Zalachenko with an axe in the woodshed, Zalachenko shot her three times with a .22 calibre Browning pistol [E3], [E4]. The first bullet struck her hip, the second hit her back and paralyzed her left arm, and the third penetrated her skull near her left ear, causing massive trauma that made her lose consciousness [E3], [E4].\n3.  **Outcome:** Salander survived the shooting but was critically injured. She later woke up in the intensive care unit at Sahlgrenska hospital in Göteborg [E5]. Niedermann fled the scene after being frightened by Salander, and Zalachenko was later killed by Gullberg at the hospital [E6], [E7].\n\nThe \"abandoned factory\" or \"brickworks\" mentioned in earlier search results refers to a different location where Niedermann hid after fleeing Gosseberga, not where Salander was shot.",
        "citations": [
          "[E1]",
          "[E2]",
          "[E3]",
          "[E4]",
          "[E5]",
          "[E6]",
          "[E7]"
        ],
        "reranked": [
          {
            "book": "The Girl Who Played with Fire",
            "book_file": "The Girl Who Played with Fire _ A Lisbeth Salander Novel -- Larsson, Stieg -- Millennium trilogy, bk_ 2, New York, 2009 -- Alfred A_ Knopf -- isbn13 9780307272300 -- 078fc916144df1e3707c069bbf984d06 -- Anna’s Archive.epub",
            "section": "The_Girl_who-ayed_with_Fire_split_074.html",
            "paragraph": 71,
            "part": 1,
            "chapter": null,
            "page": "144",
            "text": "She crawled from the grave with her eyes closed and didn’t stop until her shoulder hit a tree trunk. Slowly she turned her body so that she had the tree to lean on and wiped the dirt from her eyes with the back of her hand before she opened them. It was pitch-black around her and the air was icy cold. She was sweating. She felt a dull pain in her head, in her left shoulder, and in her hip, but didn’t spend any energy wondering why. She sat still for ten minutes, breathing. Then it came to her that she couldn’t stay there.",
            "paragraph_end": 71,
            "part_end": 1,
            "embedding_model": "sentence-transformers/multi-qa-MiniLM-L6-cos-v1",
            "source_id": "dba41cf9a902d43fd6490f714b96a7dedacf19ccb97c3e6867545c8e555739cb",
            "id": "dba41cf9-a902-d43f-d649-0f714b96a7de",
            "agent_label": "E1"
          },
          {
            "book": "The Girl Who Played with Fire",
            "book_file": "The Girl Who Played with Fire _ A Lisbeth Salander Novel -- Larsson, Stieg -- Millennium trilogy, bk_ 2, New York, 2009 -- Alfred A_ Knopf -- isbn13 9780307272300 -- 078fc916144df1e3707c069bbf984d06 -- Anna’s Archive.epub",
            "section": "The_Girl_who-ayed_with_Fire_split_074.html",
            "paragraph": 72,
            "part": 1,
            "chapter": null,
            "page": "144",
            "text": "She struggled to her feet as the world swirled around her.\nShe felt instantly sick and bent over to vomit.\nThen she started to walk. She had no idea which direction she was going. The pain in her left hip was excruciating and she kept stumbling to her knees. Each time an even greater pain shot through her head.\nShe didn’t know how long she’d been walking when she saw a light out of the corner of her eye. She changed direction. It was only when she was standing by the woodshed in the yard that she realized she had walked straight back to Zalachenko’s farmhouse. She swayed like a drunk.",
            "paragraph_end": 75,
            "part_end": 1,
            "embedding_model": "sentence-transformers/multi-qa-MiniLM-L6-cos-v1",
            "source_id": "75c677c1a011cbe0de73878eabc8414e0a9a8990bba560a3bf6d2d4e150779b1",
            "id": "75c677c1-a011-cbe0-de73-878eabc8414e",
            "agent_label": "E2"
          },
          {
            "book": "The Girl Who Played with Fire",
            "book_file": "The Girl Who Played with Fire _ A Lisbeth Salander Novel -- Larsson, Stieg -- Millennium trilogy, bk_ 2, New York, 2009 -- Alfred A_ Knopf -- isbn13 9780307272300 -- 078fc916144df1e3707c069bbf984d06 -- Anna’s Archive.epub",
            "section": "The_Girl_who-ayed_with_Fire_split_072.html",
            "paragraph": 154,
            "part": 1,
            "chapter": null,
            "page": "140",
            "text": "The fucking old man has a gun too .\nThe realization cracked like a whip through her mind.\nShe changed direction in the same instant the shot was fired. The bullet struck the outside of her hip and made her spin off balance.\nShe felt no pain.\nThe second bullet hit her in the back and stopped against her left shoulder blade. A paralyzing pain sliced through her body.\nShe went down on her knees. For a few seconds she could not move. She was conscious that Zalachenko was behind her, about twenty feet away. With one last surge of energy she stubbornly hurled herself to her feet and took a wobbly step towards the cover of the bushes.",
            "paragraph_end": 159,
            "part_end": 1,
            "embedding_model": "sentence-transformers/multi-qa-MiniLM-L6-cos-v1",
            "source_id": "90974057c3ddd98b7e65fdd3202652eb230419df7586e9fd87a328d60847e011",
            "id": "90974057-c3dd-d98b-7e65-fdd3202652eb",
            "agent_label": "E3"
          },
          {
            "book": "The Girl Who Played with Fire",
            "book_file": "The Girl Who Played with Fire _ A Lisbeth Salander Novel -- Larsson, Stieg -- Millennium trilogy, bk_ 2, New York, 2009 -- Alfred A_ Knopf -- isbn13 9780307272300 -- 078fc916144df1e3707c069bbf984d06 -- Anna’s Archive.epub",
            "section": "The_Girl_who-ayed_with_Fire_split_072.html",
            "paragraph": 160,
            "part": 1,
            "chapter": null,
            "page": "140",
            "text": "Zalachenko had time to aim.\nThe third bullet caught her about an inch below the top of her left ear. It penetrated her skull and caused a spiderweb of radial cracks in her cranium. The lead came to rest in the grey matter about two inches beneath the cerebral cortex, by the cerebrum.\nFor Salander the medical detail was academic. The bullet caused immediate massive trauma. Her last sensation was a glowing red shock that turned into a white light.",
            "paragraph_end": 162,
            "part_end": 1,
            "embedding_model": "sentence-transformers/multi-qa-MiniLM-L6-cos-v1",
            "source_id": "95082038310d0f20d644b16ea7354b422afd424f6c1d5b8a56188d91805efb4d",
            "id": "95082038-310d-0f20-d644-b16ea7354b42",
            "agent_label": "E4"
          },
          {
            "book": "The Girl Who Kicked the Hornet's Nest",
            "book_file": "The Girl Who Kicked the Hornet's Nest -- Stieg Larsson -- Millennium Trilogy #3, 2010 -- Random House, Inc_ -- a9a1c0e6f4b8fd47fa0771ff9463bc34 -- Anna’s Archive.epub",
            "section": "OEBPS/Lars_9780307593672_epub_c03_r1.htm",
            "paragraph": 86,
            "part": 1,
            "chapter": "CHAPTER 3 Friday, April 8–Saturday, April 9",
            "page": null,
            "text": "She was alive, but she was not sure whether that was a good thing.\nShe could not piece together all that had happened, but she summoned up a foggy mosaic of images from the woodshed and how she had swung an axe in fury and struck her father in the face. Zalachenko. Was he alive or dead?\nShe could not clearly remember what had happened with Niedermann. She had a memory of being surprised that he had run away, and she did not know why.",
            "paragraph_end": 88,
            "part_end": 1,
            "embedding_model": "sentence-transformers/multi-qa-MiniLM-L6-cos-v1",
            "source_id": "a4313bc9550b0156e5a75d1c1380dc18de537d8046c3d3453649c0da35813358",
            "id": "a4313bc9-550b-0156-e5a7-5d1c1380dc18",
            "agent_label": "E5"
          },
          {
            "book": "The Girl Who Kicked the Hornet's Nest",
            "book_file": "The Girl Who Kicked the Hornet's Nest -- Stieg Larsson -- Millennium Trilogy #3, 2010 -- Random House, Inc_ -- a9a1c0e6f4b8fd47fa0771ff9463bc34 -- Anna’s Archive.epub",
            "section": "OEBPS/Lars_9780307593672_epub_c07_r1.htm",
            "paragraph": 172,
            "part": 1,
            "chapter": "CHAPTER 7 Monday, April 11–Tuesday, April 12",
            "page": null,
            "text": "Zalachenko’s eyes narrowed.\n“You motherfucker,” Gullberg said then with such coldness in his voice that Zalachenko froze.\nGullberg squeezed the trigger and put the bullet right in the centre of Zalachenko’s forehead just as the patient was starting to swing his prosthesis over the edge of the bed. Zalachenko was thrown back onto the pillow. His good leg kicked four, five times before he was still. Gullberg saw a red flower-shaped splatter on the wall behind the bed. He became aware that his ears were ringing after the shot and he rubbed his left one with his free hand.",
            "paragraph_end": 174,
            "part_end": 1,
            "embedding_model": "sentence-transformers/multi-qa-MiniLM-L6-cos-v1",
            "source_id": "15676af4ed345bd2fe4ed0ef1e8dcad15fb9eabf6a23a7145f627b63e083098b",
            "id": "15676af4-ed34-5bd2-fe4e-d0ef1e8dcad1",
            "keyword_score": 12.618709896891106,
            "score": 12.618709896891106,
            "agent_label": "E6"
          },
          {
            "book": "The Girl Who Played with Fire",
            "book_file": "The Girl Who Played with Fire _ A Lisbeth Salander Novel -- Larsson, Stieg -- Millennium trilogy, bk_ 2, New York, 2009 -- Alfred A_ Knopf -- isbn13 9780307272300 -- 078fc916144df1e3707c069bbf984d06 -- Anna’s Archive.epub",
            "section": "The_Girl_who-ayed_with_Fire_split_074.html",
            "paragraph": 97,
            "part": 1,
            "chapter": null,
            "page": "144",
            "text": "At that moment she heard the stumbling approach of Niedermann, who then filled the doorway of the shed. He stopped short and registered the scene before him with uncomprehending and staring eyes. Zalachenko was wailing like a man possessed. His face was a bloody mask. He had an axe wedged in his knee. A bloody and filthy Salander was sitting on the floor next to him. She looked like something from a horror movie, and far too many of those had already played out in Niedermann’s mind.",
            "paragraph_end": 97,
            "part_end": 1,
